::ALG2_U04_L02_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
What factor represents 20% growth?
{
=A. 1.20#Correct. Growth uses 1 + 0.20 = 1.20.
~B. 0.20#This is only the rate.
~C. 0.80#This represents 20% decay.
~D. 2.00#This represents 100% growth, or doubling.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
What factor represents 7% decay?
{
~A. 1.07#This represents 7% growth.
=B. 0.93#Correct. Decay uses 1 - 0.07 = 0.93.
~C. 0.07#This is only the rate.
~D. 1.93#This does not model 7% decay.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
A value starts at 600 and grows by 3% each year. Which model fits?
{
~A. A(t)=600(0.97)^t#This models 3% decay.
~B. A(t)=1.03(600)^t#This reverses the parameters.
=C. A(t)=600(1.03)^t#Correct. The initial value is 600 and 3% growth gives factor 1.03.
~D. A(t)=600+3t#This is linear growth.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
A value starts at 900 and decreases by 4% each year. Which model fits?
{
~A. A(t)=900(1.04)^t#This models 4% growth.
~B. A(t)=0.96(900)^t#This reverses the parameters.
~C. A(t)=900-4t#This subtracts 4 units per year, not 4%.
=D. A(t)=900(0.96)^t#Correct. The initial value is 900 and 4% decay gives factor 0.96.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
In P(t)=1200(1.09)^t, what is the percent growth rate?
{
=A. 9%#Correct. A factor of 1.09 means 1 + 0.09, or 9% growth.
~B. 1.09%#The decimal part 0.09 corresponds to 9%, not 1.09%.
~C. 91%#That would match a factor of 1.91.
~D. 109%#The factor is 109% of the previous amount, which means 9% growth.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
In V(t)=5000(0.82)^t, what is the percent decay rate?
{
~A. 82%#That is the percent remaining, not the decay rate.
=B. 18%#Correct. A factor of 0.82 means 82% remains, so 18% is lost.
~C. 0.82%#This treats the factor as the percent rate.
~D. 118%#This does not represent decay from a factor of 0.82.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
What is A(2) for A(t)=100(1.5)^t?
{
~A. 150#This is A(1), not A(2).
~B. 300#This doubles the one-period increase incorrectly.
=C. 225#Correct. Compute 100 times 1.5 squared.
~D. 250#This adds 150 instead of multiplying by 1.5 twice.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
A laptop costs 1200 and loses 25% of its value each year. What is the factor?
{
~A. 1.25#This models 25% growth.
~B. 0.25#This is the decay rate, not the remaining factor.
~C. 1.75#This does not represent losing 25%.
=D. 0.75#Correct. If 25% is lost, 75% remains each year.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
A bank account grows from 1000 by 6% annually. Which model fits?
{
=A. A(t)=1000(1.06)^t#Correct. The factor 1.06 represents 6% annual growth.
~B. A(t)=1000(0.94)^t#This models 6% decay.
~C. A(t)=1.06(1000)^t#This reverses initial value and factor.
~D. A(t)=1000+6t#This adds 6 dollars per year, not 6%.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
Which factor indicates decay?
{
~A. 1.04#This indicates growth.
=B. 0.88#Correct. A factor between 0 and 1 indicates decay.
~C. 1.25#This indicates growth.
~D. 2.00#This indicates doubling growth.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
Which factor indicates growth?
{
~A. 0.15#This would shrink the amount to 15%.
~B. 0.85#This indicates decay.
=C. 1.15#Correct. A factor greater than 1 indicates growth.
~D. 0.50#This indicates decay by half.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
A quantity decays by 30%. What percent remains?
{
~A. 30%#This is the percent lost, not the percent remaining.
~B. 130%#This would represent growth.
~C. 0.30%#This confuses percent and decimal form.
=D. 70%#Correct. If 30% is lost, 70% remains.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
A quantity grows by 30%. What percent of the original is the new amount?
{
=A. 130%#Correct. Growth by 30% means the new amount is 100% plus 30%.
~B. 30%#This is only the increase.
~C. 70%#This would be decay by 30%.
~D. 0.30%#This confuses decimal and percent notation.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
What is the initial value in C(t)=750(0.95)^t?
{
~A. 0.95#This is the decay factor.
=B. 750#Correct. The coefficient is the starting amount when t = 0.
~C. 95#This is the percent remaining.
~D. 5#This is the decay rate in percent.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
What is the factor in M(t)=300(1.18)^t?
{
~A. 300#This is the initial value.
~B. 18#This is the percent growth rate, not the factor.
=C. 1.18#Correct. The factor is the repeated multiplier.
~D. 0.18#This is the decimal rate, not the full factor.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
Which model shows 12% decay from an initial amount of 40?
{
~A. y=40(1.12)^t#This models 12% growth.
~B. y=12(40)^t#This reverses parameters and uses the percent incorrectly.
~C. y=40-12t#This is linear decrease.
=D. y=40(0.88)^t#Correct. Decay by 12% means a factor of 0.88.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
Which model shows 12% growth from an initial amount of 40?
{
=A. y=40(1.12)^t#Correct. Growth by 12% means a factor of 1.12.
~B. y=40(0.88)^t#This models 12% decay.
~C. y=12(40)^t#This reverses parameters and uses the percent incorrectly.
~D. y=40+12t#This adds 12 units each period, not 12%.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
A medicine amount is modeled by M(t)=80(0.5)^t. What does 0.5 mean?
{
~A. The amount increases by half.#A factor below 1 means decay, not growth.
=B. Half remains each time period.#Correct. A factor of 0.5 means the amount is multiplied by one-half each period.
~C. The initial amount is 0.5.#The initial amount is 80.
~D. The amount loses 0.5 units.#The model loses a percent, not a fixed 0.5 units.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
A population model is P(t)=250(1.4)^t. What is the growth rate?
{
~A. 4%#That would have factor 1.04.
~B. 140%#The amount becomes 140% of previous, which means 40% growth.
=C. 40%#Correct. A factor of 1.4 means 1 + 0.4, or 40% growth.
~D. 0.4%#This confuses decimal and percent notation.
}


::ALG2_U04_L02_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U04_L02_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3
Question:
A value changes by factor 0.60 each year. What is the decay rate?
{
~A. 60%#That is the percent remaining.
~B. 6%#This misreads 0.60 as 6%.
~C. 160%#This would not describe decay.
=D. 40%#Correct. If 60% remains, 40% is lost each year.
}

